<!--
  Flatness oneloop PlotJuggler layout: ANTON_FINDI (oneloop_findi.c) and
  ANTON_FMFC (oneloop_fmfc.c), in ONE file.

  THIS LAYOUT IS SITL ONLY. READ THIS BEFORE ANYTHING ELSE.
  ========================================================
  Every series below comes from an NPS_SCOPE_VAR registration inside
  oneloop_findi.c / oneloop_fmfc.c. `nps_scope_var.h` gates the real macro on
  USE_NPS, which only the sim / nps / hitl targets define, so on an `ap` build
  those registrations compile to nothing. Neither module registers a periodic
  telemetry message either. There is therefore NO real flight log that draws
  this layout, and there is no partial fallback: on a flight log every tab here
  is blank. That is the difference from plotjuggler_mfc.xml and
  plotjuggler_indi.xml, which draw the shared pprzlink messages and work on
  both feeds.

  Transport is the same as the other layouts: one UDP Server on port 9870,
  protocol JSON, timestamp field "timestamp". `pj_json_relay.py` rewrites the
  aircraft root key to the fixed root "uav", so a firmware registration of
  "FMFC/lin/a_c_x" arrives as the series /uav/FMFC/lin/a_c_x. The slashes in
  the registration string are what build the tree; PlotJuggler does the
  splitting, the relay does not.

  WHY ONE FILE FOR TWO CONTROLLERS, AND WHY HALF OF IT IS EMPTY
  =============================================================
  Only one of the two modules links into a given aircraft, so only one prefix
  ever carries data in a given session. Every plot below therefore holds BOTH
  the FINDI/ and the FMFC/ series for the same quantity, and the other
  controller's curves stay flat empty. Curve order inside a plot is always the
  FINDI block first, then the FMFC block, in the same signal order.

  A curve whose series is absent from the datasource is expected to be skipped,
  the plot rendering with whatever it does have. plotjuggler_heol.xml is
  already in that situation, since its HEOL_NOMINAL/* and HEOL_ALPHA/* curves
  are SITL only while the rest of that layout is read off flight logs too. It
  was NOT verified for this file on a load where roughly half the curves are
  absent, because PlotJuggler cannot run in the sandbox this file was written
  in. If it turns out to complain on load rather than ignore, the fallback is
  two files sharing this header, split on the FINDI / FMFC line.

  On an ANTON_FINDI run these are empty BY DESIGN, not because the layout is
  broken:

    lin/fi_star_*         the nominal force from the flat trajectory
    ang/m_star_*          the nominal moment from the flat trajectory
    the whole est/* tree  both ultra-local estimators

  That emptiness IS the structural difference between the two controllers, and
  it is the thing worth looking at. FINDI rejects the lumped disturbance
  incrementally, on top of the wrench the actuators are currently applying:

    f_i^c = f_i^prev + m (a_c minus a_tilde)
    m_c   = m^prev  + I (dw_c minus dw_lpf)

  There is no nominal split and no F estimator anywhere in it, so there is
  nothing for those series to hold. FMFC keeps the entire FINDI spine and
  replaces ONLY those two increments with 3-channel HEOL/MFC brackets, which
  put the nominal OUTSIDE the correction and estimate the rest:

    f_i^c = f_i* + m(-F_fi - a_c)
    m_c   = m*  + I(-F_m  + dw_c)

  So an empty estimator tab on ANTON_FINDI is the expected reading. An empty
  estimator tab on ANTON_FMFC is a broken run.

  INDEX AND SIGN CONVENTIONS A READER GETS WRONG OTHERWISE
  ========================================================

  mode/flat_status IS AN ENUM, NOT A FLAG, AND ZERO IS THE GOOD VALUE.
      The other three mode series are booleans where 1 is healthy
      (guidance = the linear half ran; nom_valid = the flat table gave a
      nominal; alloc_ok = the allocator inverse is usable). flat_status is
      enum FlatnessQuadStatus from flatness_quad.h and reads the other way:
          0 = OK
          1 = FREE_FALL          |f_i^c|/m below tolerance
          2 = GIMBAL_LOCK        |cos theta| near zero with the branch OFF
          3 = BRANCH_DEGENERATE  previous body y parallel to the thrust axis
      On a degraded call the commanded attitude is the PREVIOUS call's, held,
      while `flat/f` is still the freshly computed collective. So a nonzero
      flat_status means the attitude command went stale without going flat.

  THE ANGULAR INSTRUMENTATION IS DELIBERATELY PARTIAL. Do not add series for
      the missing axes: they are not registered, and there is nothing to plot.
          ang/w_ref_    x and y only
          ang/dw_ref_   y only  (pitch)
          ang/dw_c_     y only  (pitch)
      There is no dw_c_x, no dw_c_z, no w_ref_z.

  dw_c MEANS TWO DIFFERENT THINGS ON THE TWO PREFIXES, on the one plot that
      carries dw_ref_y and dw_c_y together. FINDI forms
      dw_c = k_rate (w_c minus w) + dw_ref, so its dw_c ALREADY CONTAINS the
      feedforward. FMFC forms dw_c = k_rate (w_c minus w) with no dw_ref term
      at all; there the feedforward enters through m* = I dw_ref and nowhere
      else. Adding dw_ref back into the FMFC command would double count it. So
      the two prefixes' dw_c_y curves are not the same signal and should not be
      overlaid mentally as if they were.

  THE LINEAR ERROR SENSE IS OPPOSITE BETWEEN THE TWO MODULES, on purpose.
      FINDI's cascade uses reference minus measurement. FMFC's linear loop uses
      MEASUREMENT MINUS REFERENCE, the MFC convention. Both are wired that way
      deliberately and flipping either inverts its feedback. This is visible on
      the accel tab: for the same position error the two a_c curves point
      opposite ways, and neither is a bug.

  alloc/v IS [Mx, My, Mz, Fz], WHICH IS TWO UNITS IN ONE ARRAY. v_0 to v_2 are
      moments [N*m]; v_3 is the collective force [N]. That is why the
      Allocation tab splits them across two plots rather than drawing one
      "pseudo control" plot, which would squash the moments into a band the way
      the MFC layout squashes position against thrust.

  alloc/u AND alloc/act SHARE A UNIT but are not the same thing. Both are pprz
      counts bounded to MAX_PPRZ. u_* is the command the allocator produced
      this tick; a_* is the modelled actuator STATE, the first-order actuator
      lag applied to u_*, and it is what the applied wrench f_i^prev / m^prev
      is computed from. They get separate plots so the lag between them reads
      as a lag rather than as noise.

  flat/f IS NEGATIVE IN HOVER. It is the collective body z force in the NED
      convention where down is positive, so more thrust is more negative. Same
      for fi_c_z, fi_star_z and fi_prev_z.

  THE ESTIMATOR SERIES ARE NOT FORCES AND NOT MOMENTS.
      F_fi_0 to F_fi_2 is the force bracket's ultra-local state in [m/s^2], and
      F_m_0 to F_m_2 is the moment bracket's in [rad/s^2], because
      alpha_fi = (1/m) I and alpha_m = J^-1: F lives on the far side of alpha,
      not on the command side. Multiplying by alpha inverse is what makes du.
      du_fi_0 to du_fi_2 is [N] and du_m_0 to du_m_2 is [N*m]: the correction
      ALONE, post clamp, with the nominal not yet added.
      eps_* is the estimator DRIVE, and it is not a residual of the quantity
      the bracket commands. eps_fi_0 to eps_fi_2 is the POSITION error e_p in
      [m] (measurement minus reference). eps_m_0 to eps_m_2 is the NEGATED,
      low-pass filtered attitude error, that is -H(z) zeta_e, in [rad].
      den_fi and den_m are each bracket's shared scalar estimator denominator,
      one per bracket with a per-element numerator over it. They carry no
      physical unit, which is why they get their own plot.
      Index 0,1,2 on the force bracket is NED x,y,z. Index 0,1,2 on the moment
      bracket is BODY roll, pitch, yaw.
      This is also why the estimator lives on two tabs and not one: the four
      quantities are eight different units once the two brackets are counted
      separately, and one unit per plot is the rule this layout exists to obey.

  HOW TO TELL A BROKEN RUN FROM A TRACKING ONE
  ============================================
  Read the tabs in order. The first three checks are cheap and each one, if it
  fails, makes everything after it meaningless.

  1. mode/alloc_ok. If it drops to 0, STOP READING THE REST OF THE RUN. The
     allocator inverse failed and every command downstream of it is undefined.
     This is why the mode tab is first.
  2. mode/flat_status. Nonzero means the attitude command is a held stale one
     (see the enum above). Anything you conclude from the attitude error tab
     during that interval is about an old setpoint.
  3. Allocation, u_* and a_*. If u_* is sitting on its rails and switching
     between them, that loop is bang-banging rather than tracking, exactly the
     reading the HEOL layout's thrust and torque tabs call for. Check this
     before reading any tracking quality off the accel or force tabs.

  4. THE FORCE TAB IS THE FMFC HEALTH CHECK, and it is the reason that tab
     carries fi_star and du at all. The bracket identity is

         fi_c = fi_star + du_fi        with  du_fi = m(-F_fi - a_c)

     so at a settled hover, where a_c is near zero, du_fi is essentially
     -m F_fi. Knowledge/Sessions/2026-08-22-oneloop-fmfc-integration.md
     records this closing in flight on the z channel of the integration run:

         f_i*             = -m g       = -7.8480 N
         F_fi_2 settled                =  1.4865 m/s^2
         -m * F_fi_2                   = -1.1892 N  vs  du_fi_2 = -1.1899 N
         f_i* + du_fi_2                = -9.0379 N  vs  f       = -9.0381 N

     The correction is nonzero there for a known reason: that airframe's
     MASS = 0.8 kg is a "TODO: measure" placeholder taken from the JSBSim
     model, so the plant is about 15 percent heavier than the nominal the loop
     inverts, and the estimator absorbs the whole mismatch as a steady
     1.49 m/s^2 while hover holds. The note's own words for why this matters:
     "had the estimator been mis-signed, this is the term that would have run
     away instead of settling." So the reading is not "is F_fi_2 small" but
     "does F_fi_2 SETTLE, and does fi_star + du_fi land on fi_c". A settled,
     bounded estimate with the identity closing is the sign the estimator is
     oriented correctly. A monotone runaway on F_fi_* or du_* pinned at a rail
     is the sign it is not, and no amount of gain tuning fixes a sign.

  5. Only then, tracking: a_tilde against a_c on the accel tab, zeta_e on the
     attitude error tab, dw_lpf against dw_c_y on the angular tab.

  A CAVEAT ON READING NUMBERS OFF ANTON_FMFC AT ALL: that module is integration
  only. Its gains are the FINDI GA-tuned values with the brackets bolted on and
  never retuned, so a good tracking figure from it would be luck. Use this
  layout on FMFC to check STRUCTURE, which is what every check above is.

  PROVENANCE OF THE SERIES NAMES
  ==============================
  Every name below was taken from the NPS_SCOPE_VAR / NPS_SCOPE_VARN blocks in
  sw/airborne/firmwares/rotorcraft/oneloop/oneloop_findi.c (45 registrations)
  and oneloop_fmfc.c (71 registrations), then confirmed against two SITL
  captures on 2026-08-27, one per aircraft, by reading the column names out of
  sim_logs/. NPS_SCOPE_VARN(prefix, base, type, count) expands to
  prefix0 through prefix<count minus 1>, so "FMFC/est/F_fi_" at count 3 gives
  F_fi_0, F_fi_1, F_fi_2. If you add a registration to either module, add its
  curve here to the plot that already holds its unit rather than opening a new
  plot for it.
-->
